1
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:11,000
A Hero Will Be Revealed.

2
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:16,000
Bionicle: Mask of Light is a action, adventure,
animation film directed by David Molina.

3
00:09:15,000 --> 00:09:21,000
With darkness encroaching on the island of Mata Nui, the only hope for the survival of the islanders rests with
two Matoran villagers, who must find the seventh Toa and deliver to him the fabled Mask of Light.

4
00:12:20,000 --> 00:12:26,000
Takua / Toa Takanuva (voice), played by Jason Michas.

5
00:15:25,000 --> 00:15:31,000
Jaller (voice), played by Andrew Francis.

6
00:18:30,000 --> 00:18:36,000
Toa Tahu / Toa Onua / Graalok the Ash Bear (voice), played by Scott McNeil.

7
00:21:35,000 --> 00:21:41,000
Makuta / Pewku (voice), played by Lee Tockar.

8
00:24:40,000 --> 00:24:46,000
Turaga Vakama (voice), played by Christopher Gaze.

9
00:27:45,000 --> 00:27:51,000
Toa Gali (voice), played by Kathleen Barr.

10
00:30:50,000 --> 00:30:56,000
Bionicle: Mask of Light, stylized as BIONICLE: Mask of Light — The Movie, is a 2003 animated science fantasy action film based on the
Bionicle toyline by Lego, and particularly serves as a direct adaptation to the latter half of the toyline's 2003 narrative.

11
00:33:55,000 --> 00:34:01,000
Set in a universe filled with bio-mechanical beings allied with classical element-themed tribes, the story follows two friends from the fire-based village of
Ta-Koro on a quest to find the owner of the Mask of Light, a mystical artifact that can potentially defeat Makuta, an evil entity threatening the island.

12
00:37:00,000 --> 00:37:06,000
The project was first proposed in 2001, during the
original run of the Bionicle toyline.

13
00:40:05,000 --> 00:40:11,000
Lego contacted multiple writers for the project, including original Bionicle contributors Bob
Thompson and Alastair Swinnerton, and Hollywood writers Henry Gilroy and Greg Weisman.

14
00:43:10,000 --> 00:43:16,000
Production began in 2002, taking approximately a year to complete.

15
00:46:15,000 --> 00:46:21,000
A major part of the graphical design was adjusting the characters so they could
work in human-like ways while still resembling the original toys.

16
00:49:20,000 --> 00:49:26,000
The music was composed by Nathan Furst, who used orchestral
and tribal elements to create the score.

17
00:52:25,000 --> 00:52:31,000
Voice casting was handled by Kris Zimmerman, and the voicework was
done with the setting and mythos of Bionicle in mind.

18
00:55:30,000 --> 00:55:36,000
Multiple studios were involved in the development and distribution of Mask of Light: it was co-produced by Lego and Create TV & Film,
developed by Creative Capers Entertainment and CGCG, and post-production was handled by 310 Studios and Hacienda Post.

19
00:58:35,000 --> 00:58:41,000
It was released on September 16, 2003, on home video and DVD by Buena Vista
Home Entertainment under the Miramax Home Entertainment label.

20
01:01:40,000 --> 01:01:46,000
Upon release, the film reached high positions in VHS and DVD charts, and
received generally positive reviews from journalists.

21
01:04:45,000 --> 01:04:51,000
Mask of Light was followed by two prequel films, Bionicle 2: Legends of Metru Nui in 2004 and Bionicle 3:
Web of Shadows in 2005, and a stand-alone sequel, Bionicle: The Legend Reborn, in 2009.

22
01:07:50,000 --> 01:07:56,000
The film explores themes of mask, based on toy, best friend, quest, lego.

23
01:10:55,000 --> 01:11:01,000
The tone is dark and thought_provoking and mind_bending.
